1
00:00:00,750 --> 00:00:02,950
Same code, same machine.

2
00:00:03,530 --> 00:00:05,870
One request fails and the next one works.

3
00:00:06,630 --> 00:00:08,130
We did not replace the model.

4
00:00:08,590 --> 00:00:10,850
We changed the address we were asking the

5
00:00:10,850 --> 00:00:11,810
machine to contact.

6
00:00:12,470 --> 00:00:14,470
That is a small example of a much

7
00:00:14,470 --> 00:00:15,270
bigger habit.

8
00:00:15,870 --> 00:00:17,970
Before you ask an agent to try harder,

9
00:00:18,530 --> 00:00:20,310
figure out which part of the system can

10
00:00:20,310 --> 00:00:21,750
actually explain the failure.

11
00:00:24,283 --> 00:00:26,403
In this lesson, we will separate the model,

12
00:00:26,843 --> 00:00:29,223
the context, the tools, and the runtime.

13
00:00:30,003 --> 00:00:32,503
This is our engineering map for diagnosing a

14
00:00:32,503 --> 00:00:32,863
workflow.

15
00:00:33,543 --> 00:00:35,543
It is not a claim about four boxes

16
00:00:35,543 --> 00:00:36,663
hidden inside cursor.

17
00:00:37,263 --> 00:00:38,843
By the end, you should be able to

18
00:00:38,843 --> 00:00:40,723
look at a failed task and choose a

19
00:00:40,723 --> 00:00:44,123
useful first investigation
instead of changing everything at

20
00:00:44,123 --> 00:00:44,403
once.

21
00:00:46,783 --> 00:00:48,043
Start with the model.

22
00:00:48,583 --> 00:00:50,883
It takes the information available to it and

23
00:00:50,883 --> 00:00:53,543
produces a response, which
can include a proposed

24
00:00:53,543 --> 00:00:54,023
action.

25
00:00:54,663 --> 00:00:57,883
That proposal may be excellent,
confused, or somewhere

26
00:00:57,883 --> 00:01:00,663
in between, but a proposed command has not

27
00:01:00,663 --> 00:01:01,543
magically run.

28
00:01:02,203 --> 00:01:04,483
If the answer says, I checked the database,

29
00:01:05,043 --> 00:01:07,063
the next question is wonderfully boring.

30
00:01:07,643 --> 00:01:09,443
Where is the tool call and its result?

31
00:01:11,983 --> 00:01:15,243
Context is the information
available for that step.

32
00:01:15,823 --> 00:01:19,283
It can include instructions,
project rules, relevant files,

33
00:01:19,823 --> 00:01:22,643
your messages, and observations
returned by tools.

34
00:01:23,543 --> 00:01:24,703
Notice the word relevant.

35
00:01:25,443 --> 00:01:28,323
Giving the agent a thousand
unrelated files is

36
00:01:28,323 --> 00:01:30,063
not the same as giving it the permission

37
00:01:30,063 --> 00:01:31,123
rule it needs.

38
00:01:31,503 --> 00:01:34,263
A missing requirement can make
a beautifully implemented

39
00:01:34,263 --> 00:01:35,723
feature the wrong feature.

40
00:01:38,083 --> 00:01:41,663
Tools connect the conversation
to actions and observations.

41
00:01:42,543 --> 00:01:45,683
Reading a file, searching a
repository, editing code,

42
00:01:46,023 --> 00:01:48,323
and running a shell command
are different operations.

43
00:01:49,103 --> 00:01:51,763
Each has inputs, an execution path, and a

44
00:01:51,763 --> 00:01:52,583
result to inspect.

45
00:01:53,383 --> 00:01:55,543
A tool refusing an action is also a

46
00:01:55,543 --> 00:01:55,843
result.

47
00:01:56,403 --> 00:01:57,883
It is not the same event as the

48
00:01:57,883 --> 00:01:59,863
application running and returning an error.

49
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:05,130
The runtime is where an
action actually happens.

50
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:07,210
Which checkout is open?

51
00:02:07,730 --> 00:02:08,970
What is the working directory?

52
00:02:09,710 --> 00:02:11,350
Is the executable installed?

53
00:02:12,010 --> 00:02:13,250
Is the database running?

54
00:02:13,810 --> 00:02:15,690
Can this machine reach that address?

55
00:02:16,130 --> 00:02:18,770
A brilliant explanation of a test does not

56
00:02:18,770 --> 00:02:20,090
put node on the path.

57
00:02:20,790 --> 00:02:23,570
Sometimes the most productive
next move is checking

58
00:02:23,570 --> 00:02:26,230
one very ordinary environmental assumption.

59
00:02:28,683 --> 00:02:31,043
These layers influence one another.

60
00:02:32,003 --> 00:02:33,483
Instructions guide how the agent works.

61
00:02:34,083 --> 00:02:36,903
Tools can bring new
information into context, and

62
00:02:36,903 --> 00:02:39,803
the runtime determines what
an executed command can

63
00:02:39,803 --> 00:02:40,223
observe.

64
00:02:41,163 --> 00:02:43,283
Cursor coordinates the model and its tools.

65
00:02:44,043 --> 00:02:46,023
Our job is to make the objective and

66
00:02:46,023 --> 00:02:49,423
constraints clear, then inspect
enough of that interaction

67
00:02:49,423 --> 00:02:51,583
to know why the next step makes sense.

68
00:02:54,050 --> 00:02:56,590
Let us make the map concrete in cursor.

69
00:02:57,430 --> 00:02:59,530
We are using the operations hub server from

70
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:00,430
the previous lesson.

71
00:03:01,110 --> 00:03:02,850
The question is deliberately narrow.

72
00:03:03,570 --> 00:03:06,750
Trace the health endpoint,
identify the database operation,

73
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:09,750
and explain what happens
if that operation throws.

74
00:03:10,510 --> 00:03:12,490
We are asking for an explanation of the

75
00:03:12,490 --> 00:03:13,410
current implementation.

76
00:03:14,130 --> 00:03:16,170
We are not asking the agent to modify

77
00:03:16,170 --> 00:03:16,710
the service.

78
00:03:19,183 --> 00:03:21,343
the model controls are
worth reading carefully.

79
00:03:22,083 --> 00:03:25,043
In this recorded setup,
the menu identifies Cursor

80
00:03:25,043 --> 00:03:28,143
Grok 4.6 as the model, while High is

81
00:03:28,143 --> 00:03:28,863
the Effort setting.

82
00:03:29,503 --> 00:03:30,483
Those are different choices.

83
00:03:31,323 --> 00:03:33,563
Available labels can change
with the product and

84
00:03:33,563 --> 00:03:34,083
your account.

85
00:03:34,943 --> 00:03:37,963
Learn to inspect the selected
configuration rather than

86
00:03:37,963 --> 00:03:40,263
assuming every word beside the chat box is

87
00:03:40,263 --> 00:03:40,903
a model name.

88
00:03:43,183 --> 00:03:46,403
Here, I attach the actual server file with

89
00:03:46,403 --> 00:03:47,143
an at mention.

90
00:03:47,703 --> 00:03:50,203
That makes the relevant
source explicit in the

91
00:03:50,203 --> 00:03:50,583
request.

92
00:03:51,283 --> 00:03:53,223
If I did not know the location, I

93
00:03:53,223 --> 00:03:55,083
could ask the agent to search first.

94
00:03:55,803 --> 00:03:58,083
Attaching a file does not mean every other

95
00:03:58,083 --> 00:04:00,763
input disappears, and it does not prove the

96
00:04:00,763 --> 00:04:02,123
agent will reason correctly.

97
00:04:02,123 --> 00:04:04,603
It gives us a specific piece of context

98
00:04:04,603 --> 00:04:05,463
we can point to.

99
00:04:07,750 --> 00:04:09,490
Read the rest of the prompt too.

100
00:04:10,130 --> 00:04:12,770
We ask for file and line references, forbid

101
00:04:12,770 --> 00:04:15,730
edits and command execution
for this particular task,

102
00:04:16,030 --> 00:04:17,310
and keep the response short.

103
00:04:17,950 --> 00:04:19,930
The important part is the bounded objective.

104
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:23,210
We want an explanation
grounded in this file.

105
00:04:23,770 --> 00:04:25,650
We are not turning a small reading question

106
00:04:25,650 --> 00:04:28,070
into a request to redesign the application.

107
00:04:30,550 --> 00:04:33,830
The response identifies the
health route, the small

108
00:04:33,830 --> 00:04:36,030
database query, and the error response.

109
00:04:36,670 --> 00:04:38,750
We can compare that directly with the source

110
00:04:38,750 --> 00:04:39,330
on the left.

111
00:04:40,070 --> 00:04:42,530
The handler waits for the
query before returning

112
00:04:42,530 --> 00:04:42,970
success.

113
00:04:43,690 --> 00:04:46,430
The catch block sets a
service unavailable response

114
00:04:46,430 --> 00:04:49,070
when something inside the
protected block throws.

115
00:04:49,830 --> 00:04:52,370
This is an explanation
we can inspect against

116
00:04:52,370 --> 00:04:53,190
the current code.

117
00:04:55,750 --> 00:04:58,950
Now, expand the activity
instead of stopping at

118
00:04:58,950 --> 00:04:59,970
the polished answer.

119
00:05:00,570 --> 00:05:02,850
This run shows a file read, a project

120
00:05:02,850 --> 00:05:04,790
skill, and a repository search.

121
00:05:05,290 --> 00:05:07,470
That is useful evidence about how the answer

122
00:05:07,470 --> 00:05:08,070
was assembled.

123
00:05:08,670 --> 00:05:10,630
It also reminds us that our typed message

124
00:05:10,630 --> 00:05:12,630
was not the only instruction in play.

125
00:05:12,630 --> 00:05:15,870
Project guidance can shape
the workflow around the

126
00:05:15,870 --> 00:05:16,150
model.

127
00:05:18,550 --> 00:05:20,750
There is a limit to this result.

128
00:05:21,690 --> 00:05:24,730
Reading the handler and
describing its catch block

129
00:05:24,730 --> 00:05:27,690
is not a runtime test of the failure

130
00:05:27,690 --> 00:05:28,130
path.

131
00:05:28,830 --> 00:05:30,990
Keep two kinds of evidence separate.

132
00:05:32,110 --> 00:05:35,270
Code inspection tells us
about a possible execution

133
00:05:35,270 --> 00:05:35,870
path.

134
00:05:36,570 --> 00:05:39,650
A runtime check tells us
what actually happened

135
00:05:39,650 --> 00:05:40,990
during an execution.

136
00:05:41,750 --> 00:05:44,050
Both matter, and each has limits.

137
00:05:46,817 --> 00:05:49,677
The simplified round trip looks like this.

138
00:05:50,137 --> 00:05:51,857
A request supplies the question.

139
00:05:52,417 --> 00:05:54,477
The model proposes a tool action.

140
00:05:55,137 --> 00:05:58,177
The tool executes under its
configured conditions and

141
00:05:58,177 --> 00:05:59,357
returns an observation.

142
00:06:00,097 --> 00:06:03,257
That observation becomes
information for the next decision.

143
00:06:03,897 --> 00:06:06,417
A useful agent can revise its approach from

144
00:06:06,417 --> 00:06:06,957
that result.

145
00:06:07,337 --> 00:06:09,997
If the result is hidden
or misunderstood, the

146
00:06:09,997 --> 00:06:12,437
next step is built on a shaky foundation.

147
00:06:14,983 --> 00:06:17,603
Now we move from reading code to checking

148
00:06:17,603 --> 00:06:18,203
the machine.

149
00:06:18,843 --> 00:06:21,063
In this terminal, the
working directory is the

150
00:06:21,063 --> 00:06:23,703
operations hub checkout
and node is available.

151
00:06:24,523 --> 00:06:27,243
Those are modest observations,
but they remove two

152
00:06:27,243 --> 00:06:28,683
possible sources of confusion.

153
00:06:29,283 --> 00:06:31,723
A command run from a different directory or

154
00:06:31,723 --> 00:06:34,223
through a different terminal
environment might not see

155
00:06:34,223 --> 00:06:35,983
the same files or executables.

156
00:06:38,517 --> 00:06:41,917
For this demonstration, I
deliberately request the health

157
00:06:41,917 --> 00:06:44,017
endpoint on port 3101.

158
00:06:44,857 --> 00:06:46,997
Our service is configured on 3100.

159
00:06:47,697 --> 00:06:49,497
The first request fails to connect.

160
00:06:50,117 --> 00:06:53,137
This is an intentional
wrong address exercise, not

161
00:06:53,137 --> 00:06:54,837
a spontaneous production incident.

162
00:06:55,457 --> 00:06:57,837
We have not changed the
application, stopped the

163
00:06:57,837 --> 00:07:00,177
database, or asked the
model to repair anything

164
00:07:00,177 --> 00:07:00,457
yet.

165
00:07:02,850 --> 00:07:04,850
What does that failure establish?

166
00:07:05,490 --> 00:07:07,770
The client did not establish a connection to

167
00:07:07,770 --> 00:07:09,670
the address it tried at that moment.

168
00:07:10,250 --> 00:07:12,250
It does not establish that the handler has

169
00:07:12,250 --> 00:07:14,150
a bug because we did not get as

170
00:07:14,150 --> 00:07:16,650
far as executing that handler
through this request.

171
00:07:17,410 --> 00:07:20,810
Before rewriting code, compare
the requested address with

172
00:07:20,810 --> 00:07:23,270
the address the service
is actually configured to

173
00:07:23,270 --> 00:07:23,590
use.

174
00:07:25,983 --> 00:07:28,843
Change the request to
the configured port and

175
00:07:28,843 --> 00:07:29,523
run it again.

176
00:07:30,323 --> 00:07:32,963
Now the health response
comes back, including the

177
00:07:32,963 --> 00:07:34,303
database connection status.

178
00:07:35,063 --> 00:07:38,523
In this controlled example,
correcting the address explains

179
00:07:38,523 --> 00:07:39,803
the difference we observed.

180
00:07:40,423 --> 00:07:43,403
We keep both results, the failed attempt and

181
00:07:43,403 --> 00:07:44,383
the successful check.

182
00:07:44,883 --> 00:07:46,903
The failed attempt is part of the diagnosis,

183
00:07:47,563 --> 00:07:49,323
not something embarrassing to erase.

184
00:07:51,683 --> 00:07:54,263
Notice what we did not need here, a

185
00:07:54,263 --> 00:07:55,263
tournament of models.

186
00:07:55,903 --> 00:07:59,023
We needed a hypothesis
about the environment, one

187
00:07:59,023 --> 00:08:01,443
changed input, and another observation.

188
00:08:02,243 --> 00:08:04,503
That does not mean models
never make reasoning

189
00:08:04,503 --> 00:08:04,923
mistakes.

190
00:08:05,563 --> 00:08:07,883
It means a model comparison is a poor

191
00:08:07,883 --> 00:08:10,723
substitute for checking an
address when the evidence

192
00:08:10,723 --> 00:08:12,683
already points to an address problem.

193
00:08:15,150 --> 00:08:17,110
Context has a budget too.

194
00:08:18,150 --> 00:08:22,330
Files, instructions, conversation,
and tool results consume space,

195
00:08:22,710 --> 00:08:24,990
and long conversations may be summarized.

196
00:08:25,810 --> 00:08:28,510
Do not build your workflow
around one remembered

197
00:08:28,510 --> 00:08:30,490
capacity number for every model.

198
00:08:31,130 --> 00:08:34,690
Preserve the requirements,
decisions, and useful evidence where

199
00:08:34,690 --> 00:08:36,190
the next step can recover them.

200
00:08:36,590 --> 00:08:39,430
We will explore cursor
context controls in detail

201
00:08:39,430 --> 00:08:41,130
when we work on larger changes.

202
00:08:43,483 --> 00:08:46,143
The same reasoning applies
when work moves to

203
00:08:46,143 --> 00:08:46,903
a cloud agent.

204
00:08:47,423 --> 00:08:50,223
The remote machine needs its
own checkout, dependencies,

205
00:08:50,703 --> 00:08:52,483
configuration, and running services.

206
00:08:53,163 --> 00:08:55,323
A file saved only on your laptop does

207
00:08:55,323 --> 00:08:57,763
not become a remote file because both agents

208
00:08:57,763 --> 00:08:58,583
can discuss it.

209
00:08:59,163 --> 00:09:02,023
When a result differs across
environments, compare the

210
00:09:02,023 --> 00:09:04,823
inputs and machine state
before deciding which layer

211
00:09:04,823 --> 00:09:05,423
is responsible.

212
00:09:07,983 --> 00:09:11,423
Your checkpoint is to choose
a first investigation.

213
00:09:12,103 --> 00:09:14,983
Not to pretend every failure has exactly one

214
00:09:14,983 --> 00:09:15,383
cause.

215
00:09:16,123 --> 00:09:17,903
A terminal cannot find node.

216
00:09:18,543 --> 00:09:21,023
An agent implements a feature
without a requirement

217
00:09:21,023 --> 00:09:22,163
it was never given.

218
00:09:22,803 --> 00:09:25,683
The tool boundary refuses an edit, and an

219
00:09:25,683 --> 00:09:28,463
agent draws a conclusion
broader than its evidence.

220
00:09:29,203 --> 00:09:31,523
Pause and assign a starting layer to each

221
00:09:31,523 --> 00:09:31,823
case.

222
00:09:40,317 --> 00:09:43,377
For the missing executable,
start with the runtime.

223
00:09:44,257 --> 00:09:47,317
For the missing requirement,
inspect the context and

224
00:09:47,317 --> 00:09:48,397
clarify the behavior.

225
00:09:49,157 --> 00:09:52,057
For the refused edit, inspect the action and

226
00:09:52,057 --> 00:09:52,977
its permitted scope.

227
00:09:53,697 --> 00:09:56,997
For the unsupported conclusion,
challenge the reasoning and

228
00:09:56,997 --> 00:09:58,257
ask for an evidence map.

229
00:09:58,957 --> 00:10:00,797
These are starting points for investigation.

230
00:10:01,377 --> 00:10:03,597
They are not verdicts that rule out every

231
00:10:03,597 --> 00:10:04,797
other contributing factor.

232
00:10:07,217 --> 00:10:10,457
The next check should reduce
a specific uncertainty.

233
00:10:11,457 --> 00:10:13,537
Which executable can this terminal find?

234
00:10:14,277 --> 00:10:16,697
Which permission requirement
reached the conversation?

235
00:10:17,597 --> 00:10:19,577
Was the requested tool action allowed?

236
00:10:20,397 --> 00:10:22,497
Which observation supports that conclusion?

237
00:10:23,357 --> 00:10:26,097
You can ask those questions
calmly and precisely.

238
00:10:26,777 --> 00:10:29,237
A longer prompt with more
exclamation points does

239
00:10:29,237 --> 00:10:30,917
not make the missing evidence appear.

240
00:10:33,317 --> 00:10:35,277
Keep this map beside you,

241
00:10:35,957 --> 00:10:36,857
Model, Context,

242
00:10:37,777 --> 00:10:38,797
Tools, Runtime.

243
00:10:39,557 --> 00:10:43,177
Read the actual request,
inspect the actual action,

244
00:10:43,577 --> 00:10:45,617
and connect the result to the place it

245
00:10:45,617 --> 00:10:45,937
ran.

246
00:10:46,677 --> 00:10:48,657
That gives you a much better starting point

247
00:10:48,657 --> 00:10:49,937
when an agent gets stuck.

248
00:10:50,937 --> 00:10:53,477
Next, we will decide who
owns the requirements,

249
00:10:53,977 --> 00:10:57,657
the architectural choices, the
implementation, and the final

250
00:10:57,657 --> 00:10:59,137
judgment that a change is ready.
